  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Noticeably cooler/pleasant and rain-free start to\nthe week \n\nRefreshing airmass inbound behind cold front this morning with\ndewpoints in the 50s across northwest PA. Upslope stratus will \ndissipate over the Laurel Highlands early this morning with a\ngeneral decreasing cloud trend and plenty of sunshine expected \nthrough the afternoon. Max temps will be 10 to 15 degrees \nlower/cooler vs. Sunday ranging from the mid 60s in the northern\ntier mtns to the mid 70s in the southern valleys along the MD \nline. \n\nStrong radiational cooling pattern tonight into early Tuesday \nmorning with very low pwats beneath area of high pressure \nmigrating over CPA. Min temps with 3-handles are definitely in \nplay in the usual northern tier cold spots with 40-50F lows \nanticipated elsewhere (4-8F below climo). This setup favors \nvalley fog formation with localized visibility restrictions \nbelow 1 mile. \n\nWide 25-35 degree diurnal range fcst Tuesday with one more \npleasant and comfortable afternoon with max temps in the upper \n60s to mid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warm/humid conditions return mid to late week \nwith best odds for rain Thursday \n\nReturn SSW flow on the back side of the high pressure system \nwill pump the dewpoints and moisture/humidity back up from mid \nto late week. Best rain/QPF signal at this time is on Thursday\n9/17 as a weak front sinks southward from the eastern Great \nLakes. If the front can push south of the MD line, most of \nFriday could be dry. Precip odds then steadily increase over the\nweekend ahead of next frontal system progression from the Great\nLakes. Large scale upper level ridging should support a mid to \nlate week warming trend with the largest hi/lo departure from \nclimo Wed-Thu.",
